You don't bind in firmware — you match a name. Create the widget on the dashboard bound
to, say, `light`, then write a handler for the same name on the device:

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("light") {
  digitalWrite(LIGHT_PIN, value.asBool());
}
```

The shared variable name is the binding. There is no registration call.

Not by hand — the library acks each write as it delivers it to your handler. Acking is
how the cloud learns the write landed so it can stop re-sending.

Because delivery is at-least-once, your handler can run twice for the same command. Keep
it idempotent: set state from `value` rather than toggling.

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("relay") {
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, value.asBool());
}
```

That is safe to run repeatedly. A handler that flipped the pin would not be.

